## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development the of global economy, the energy and environmental crisis has arrived [1–4]. Due to electric energy being an essential green and clean energy type in modern society, the efficient storage modes of electric energy have attracted extensive attention and research [5–7]. Supercapacitor is a novel energy conversion device, which can convert chemical energy into electrical energy [8]. However, their low energy densities have hindered the development of supercapacitors [9]. The hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) at the electrodes are the key to the energy conversion mechanism in supercapacitors. Therefore, it is of high importance to improve the efficiency of both the HER and OER [10–14]. Nowadays, the commercial electrode in electrocatalysts is the Pt-C electrode, its disadvantages such as high cost and agglomeration, have inhibited the development of noble metal electrodes [15,16]. It is urgent to design an alternative efficient and low-cost electrode material to solve the aforementioned problems.
Lanthanide metals have excellent application potential in catalysis. For instance, Tasleem et al. prepared bimetallic Co-La modified 3D $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ to promote hydrogen emission [7]. However, due to research on lanthanide metals as a single central element of MOF remaining scarce, we selected a single lanthanide as the metal element in a MOF to study the triple-function electrode material. Ce is the most naturally abundant rare earth element, and has unique electronic properties due to the 4f hierarchy of lanthanides [29–32]. Therefore, in this study, Ce was selected as the metal central ion in a 2D MOF. Then, Pt nanoparticles were uniformly anchored on the surface of the 2D Ce-MOF to produce CeMOF@Pt composites [33]. The preparation process is simple and contains only trace amounts of Pt nanoparticles, which greatly reduces the synthesis cost of the material and improves its economic benefit. This composite can strongly couple Pt nanoparticles with the Ce-MOF material, not only preventing the agglomeration of nanoparticles, but also increasing the number of reactive active sites. Meanwhile, the two valence states in the Ce-MOF can easily realize the conversion between ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ and accelerate the rate of ion gain and loss, thus, promoting the electrochemical reaction in the electrolyte. The work presented in this paper shows high potential for application in further improving the energy storage capacities and electrocatalytic performance of supercapacitors.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials synthesis
Cerium nitrate $(\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99.99\%$ , 3AChem), 1, 3, 5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{T}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ , $98\%$ , Macklin), chloroplatinic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}P t C l_{6}}$ , 8 wt% in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , Macklin), and ethanol (EtOH, AR, Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd) were commercially purchased and used without further purification treatment.
2.1.1. Synthesis of $C e$ -MOF.
$\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ ( $25\ \mathrm{mmol};$ was added to $500~\mathrm{mL}$ of a $1{:}1\ \mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}$ mixed solution of deionised water and ethanol. Then, $\mathrm{T}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ $(25~\mathrm{mmol})$ ) was added and the resulting solution stirred via ultrasound at room temperature for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , resulting in a white cloudy liquid. The product was washed alternately with ethanol and deionised water. The washed Ce-MOF was further dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under vacuum to obtain the white Ce-MOF powder.
2.1.2. Fabrication of Ce-MOF@Pt.
In order to synthesise the Pt compound composite on the surface of the Ce-MOF, a simple one-step hydrothermal process was used. Ce-MOF $\mathrm{\nabla}50~\mathrm{mg})$ was dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol, followed by addition of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}$ solution $(100\upmu\mathrm{L}0.1\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ , and then, magnetically stirred at room temperature for 1 h. Then the temperature was raised from room temperature to $80~^{\circ}C$ in a water bath and kept there for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ while stirring continuously. When heated, the white turbid liquid gradually turned grey. When the turbid liquid was cooled to room temperature, the product was alternately washed with ethanol and deionised water, three times each. Finally, the turbid liquid was kept in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain the grey ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.10}}$ powder. Similarly, when the concentration of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}$ solution is adjusted to $0.05~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and $0.15~\mathrm{\mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ while keeping all other conditions remain unchanged, Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 and Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 were obtained, respectively.
## 2.2. Materials characterisation
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurements
## 2.3.1. Preparation of electrodes
The sample material $(80\%)$ , carbon black $(10\%)$ and polyethylene difluoride $(10\%)$ were mixed and magnetically stirred for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and the obtained mud was coated on dried nickel foam ( ${\mathrm{.1~cm}}\times1{\mathrm{~cm}}{\mathrm{.}}$ , followed by vacuum drying at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . The active substance loading on nickel foam was approximately $2.5~\mathrm{mg}$ . The working electrode was prepared.
## 2.3.2. Supercapacitor performance
The three-electrode system consisted of a working electrode (active material), a reference electrode $({\mathrm{Hg/Hg0}})$ , a counter electrode (Pt foil), and $3\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH as the electrolyte. Cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge discharge (GCD) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), were tested at the electrochemical workstation (CHI 660E). The performance test result is the average of the three tests. The specific capacitance (C) was estimated according to equation (1):
$$
C=\frac{I\times\Delta t}{m\times\Delta V}
$$
where ${\mathsf{C}}({\mathsf{F}}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1})$ represents the specific capacity, I (A) represents the constant discharge current, Dt (s) represents the discharge time of GCD, m (g) is the mass of the active materials, and V (V) represents the voltage range of GCD. EIS curves were conducted from $0.01{\mathrm{Hz}}$ to $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ . The rate property was obtained by repetitive galvanostatic charge–discharge under a disparate current density. The long-term cycling was conducted by galvanostatic charge–discharge in the battery tester (Neware, BTS $7.6\mathrm{x})$ . The above methods were used to evaluate the supercapacitor properties of the active substances.
## 2.3.3. Electrocatalytic performance
The three-electrode system consisted of a working electrode (active material), a reference electrode $\mathrm{(Hg/HgO)}$ , a counter electrode (Pt foil), and 1 M KOH as the electrolyte. HER, OER, Tafel and cycling stability were tested using an electrochemical workstation $\mathrm{'CHI660E)}$ . The polarization curves of HER and OER were determined using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV). A reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) was used to calibrate all the potentials, and it was deduced that $\mathrm{E_{RHE}=E_{H g/H g O}+0.0591p H+0.098}$ . The performance test result is the average of the three tests.
## 2.3.4. Relating calculation
(a) The relationship between peak current, scanning rate and reactant diffusivity can be expressed as equation (2) [34]:
$$
i_{p}/V^{1/2}=(2.69\times10^{5})n^{3/2}{S D_{0}}^{1/2}{C_{0}}^{0}
$$
Where i represents the peak current (A), V represents the scanning rate $(\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ , n represents the number of electrons gained and lost, S represents the actual area of the electrode $(\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ , ${\sf D}_{0}$ represents the diffusity of the reactant $(\mathsf{c m}^{2}\ \mathsf{s}^{-1})$ , and ${\sf C}_{0}$ represents the initial concentration of the reactant $(\mathsf{m o l}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ).
(b) The relationship between the peak current (i) and the scanning rate (v) was used to further study the kinetics of the electrochemical reaction. The calculation of slope B follows equations (3) and (4) [35]:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{i=a\upsilon^{b}}}\\ {{\phantom{}}}\\ {{l o g i=l o g a+b l o g\upsilon}}\end{array}
$$
where, i is the current density, $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}}$ is scanning rate, and a and b are constants. When slope b is less than or equal to 0.5, this indicates that the process is diffusion control led. When the slope b is in the range of $0.5\leq\mathsf{b}<1.0$ this indicates that the process is both diffusion and capacitance-controlled. When slope b is greater than or equal to 1.0, the process is capacitance-controlled.
(c) The calculation of capacitance contribution follows equation (5) [36]:
$$
i=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}
$$
Where, $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ represents the capacitance-controlled process and $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ represents the diffusion-controlled process.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Material characterisation
Herein, 2D Ce-MOF@Pt composites were successfully synthesised via a simple strategy, as shown in Scheme 1. During the synthesis process, $\mathrm{T}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ was used as the ligand for ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ , and the dispersed ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ species were trapped by the oxygen atoms of the carboxyl group at room temperature to obtain ‘‘ice cream stick”– 2D Ce-MOF. The molecular formula of 2D Ce-MOF nanorods is Ce (BTC) $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}$ (CCDC: 290771) [37]. In this structure, the cerium ion binds with oxygen atoms in three carboxyl groups, which have the ability to form coordination bonds Oxygen atoms on the carboxyl group bond with cerium ions, whose high coordination number improves the stability of the MOF. Then, Pt was nucleated onto 2D Ce-MOF using a simple hydrothermal method, and the 2D CeMOF@Pt was synthesized. Pt has high catalytic activity and stability, and the addition of Pt onto the surface of MOF can effectively improve their conductivity of MOF promote electron transmission [38].
SEM shows that the morphology of Ce-MOF resembles a ‘‘straw bundle” structure composed of nanorods (Fig. 1a). The surface of the Ce-MOF is smooth (Fig. 1b). Due to the introduction of Pt at the Ce-MOF surface, the bundles grew larger (Fig. 1c), and the roughness of the Ce-MOF@Pt surface also clearly increased (Fig. 1d). With increasing Pt concentration, the roughness of Ce$\mathsf{M O F@P t-0.10}$ and Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 increases gradually (Figure S1). Meanwhile, the length of Ce-MOF@Pt does not change significantly, indicating that the Ce-MOF structure is stable. Fig. 1e shows a typical TEM image of straw bundles of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05. When the image is further enlarged, well-dispersed Pt nanoparticles can be seen embedded in the Ce-MOF rods (Fig. 1f, Figure S2a). The crystal lattice fringes of Pt can be clearly observed in HRTEM images, and the spacing of the fringes is approximately $0.232\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The Pt nanoparticles are uniformly dispersed without agglomeration. The Pt nanoparticles size is about $3-5\ \mathrm{~nm}$ (Fig. 1g, Figure $S2({\mathsf{b}}{-}{\mathsf{e}}).$ . Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) indicates that C, O, Ce and Pt are also distributed in CeMOF@Pt (Fig. 1h). Simultaneously, the mapping spectrum of Ce$\mathsf{M O F@P t-0.05}$ shows that the Pt peak area is very small and the mass percentage is only $2.6\%$ , indicating that the Pt content in Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 is low (Figure S3). In the SAED pattern the obvious spots are assigned to the (1 1 1), (2 0 0), and (2 2 0) planes of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05, indicating that the Pt on the surface of CeMOF@Pt-0.05 has a polycrystalline structure (Fig. 1i). The corresponding mapping image (Fig. 1j) also shows that Ce-MOF is uniformly covered by C, O, Ce, and Pt.
In order to analyse the structure and composition of the samples, XRD analyses were carried out on Ce-MOF, Ce-MOF@Pt0.05, Ce-MOF@Pt-0.10, and ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.15}}$ , and indicated that Ce-MOF was successfully synthesised (Fig. 2a) [37]. The XRD peaks are sharp, indicating that the crystallinity of the four samples is high. Fig. 2b shows the TGA curve of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05. When the temperature reaches $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the water molecules in Ce-MOF@Pt0.05 begin to break down. When the temperature rises to $189^{\circ}\mathrm{{^C}}$ (Figure S4), the six water molecules are completely broken down, resulting in a weight loss of $19.66\ \%$ ; The organic ligands of CeMOF@Pt-0.05 began to decompose at $543^{\circ}\mathrm{~\vec{C}~},$ , and the organic ligands completely decomposed by approximately $650^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S4) with a weight loss of $20.08\%$ [37]. The TGA plots of CeMOF, Ce-MOF@Pt-0.10, and Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 were similar to that of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 (Figure S5), thus the structural stability of the synthesized MOF is due to the high coordination number of cerium ions. Meanwhile, an absorption band at $3403.8~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was observed in the FT-IR spectrum due to the asymmetric stretching vibrations of $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bonds, an absorption band at $761.8~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appears due to the bending vibration of the $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bond, and an absorption peak at $534.2~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appears due to the presence of Ce-O [39]. The absence of absorption bands at $1720\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicates complete deprotonation of $\mathtt{T}_{3}\mathtt{B T C}$ (Fig. 2d) [40]. The stretching vibrations and bending vibration of the carboxyl groups $({\mathsf{C}}{=}0)$ 1 leads to two absorption bands at 1612.2, 1558.2, 1436.7, and $1373.1~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively (Fig. 2d). The coordination mode of $\mathrm{T}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ with ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ is chelating bidentate or bridging bidentate due to $\Delta\nu=175.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}<200~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [41,42]. This corresponds to the previous molecular cell structure diagram. The IR spectra of the four samples are similar, indicating that the introduction of Pt on the surface of the Ce-MOF does not change the original structure, which further proves the high stability of the Ce-MOF. BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) analysis is performed to obtain the specific surface areas of the sample. The specific surfaces of Ce-MOF, Ce$\mathsf{M O F@P t-0.05}$ , Ce-MOF@Pt-0.10 and Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 are 54.4, 60.6, 67.3 and $31.6\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure S6). It indicates that appropriate amount of Pt NPs can increase the specific surface area of the sample, but excessive Pt NPs causes a blockage of CeMOF surface, resulting in a smaller specific surface area.
In order to further analyse the electronic structure of Ce$\mathsf{M O F@P t}$ , XPS characterization was carried out. All XPS profiles are calibrated from the C1s peak at $284.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ . It can be seen from the total XPS spectrum that the Ce-MOF does not contain Pt, while Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05, Ce-MOF@Pt-0.10 and Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 all contain Pt, further indicating that Pt has been successfully added onto the Ce-MOF (Figure S8). Figure S9 shows binding energies of 288.6, 286.7 and $284.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to $0{\cdot}C{=}0$ , CAO and CAC $(s p^{2})$ bonding, respectively [43]. At 286.7 eV, the peak area of CeMOF@Pt-0.05 decreased compared with that of Ce-MOF, while the same peaks are absent in $\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.10}$ and Ce-MOF@Pt0.15, indicating that the oxygen-containing functional groups on the carboxyl group gradually reduced with increasing Pt content [44]. After calculating the XPS peak areas, it was found that the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group of the Ce-MOF accounted for $8.89\%$ of the total C area, while $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 accounted for only $7.15\%$ . Figure S10 also confirmed that with increasing Pt content, the peak area of organic CAO (533.0 eV) decreased, indicating that the number of oxygen-containing functional groups decreased. The peak at 531.5 eV corresponds to the surface chemisorbed oxygen $(0_{\mathrm{c}})$ [43,45]. The Ce 3d peaks were found at 906.0, 885.5 900.7 and $882.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , attributed to $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}3\mathsf{d}_{5/2}$ , $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}3\mathsf{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{C e}^{4+}3\mathsf{d}^{5/2}$ , ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , respectively, indicating that the four samples contained both ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ [46] (Figure S11). The electrical conductivity of CeMOF is enhanced by the rapid transfer of electrons between the two oxidation states of cerium. Peaks at 75.2, 74.7, 72.1 and $71.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ and Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ , respectively. Among these, the valence state of Pt at the peaks $75.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $72.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ is $^+2$ $(\mathsf{P t}^{2+})$ , while the XPS binding energy at 74.7 and $71.4\mathrm{eV}$ indicate that part of Pt in Ce-MOF@Pt exists in the form of Pt atoms $(\mathsf{P t}^{0})$ (Fig. 3). Compared with the characteristic peak of pure Pt (74.25 and $70.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ [47], the XPS binding energies of $\mathrm{Pt}$ in the metallic state are positively shifted at 74.7 and $71.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which is mainly due to the change of the atoms’chemical environment, the existence of electron transfer between Ce and Pt, and the interaction between them.
## 3.2. Performance testing
MOFs have a wide range of applications in electrocatalysis due to their high density and uniformly dispersed catalytic active sites [48]. Pt is a highly efficient electrochemical catalyst because of its high catalytic activity surface; due to this, the HER and OER activities of the four samples were measured. With increasing Pt content the overpotential for OER clearly decreases, and the overpotential of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 is as low as $340~\mathrm{mV}.$ . Similarly, the Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 HER has the lowest overpotential of $208~\mathrm{{\mV}}$ (Fig. 4a, 4d). The results indicated that the electrocatalytic activity of Ce-MOF@Pt can be effectively changed by adjusting the Pt content. The cyclic stability of the OER at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and the HER at current density of $-10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ were studied by chronopotentiometry analysis. The overpotentials of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.10 and ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.15}}$ are lower than that of CeMOF. As shown in Fig. 4a–b, the OER performance of Ce$\mathrm{MOF@Pt-0.15}$ is maintained at $98.70\%$ , and the HER performance is maintained at $95.98\%$ after $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ cycling, showing remarkable durability and stability. Thus, ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.15}}$ can be used as an efficient OER and HER electrocatalyst for with highly durable properties. Fig. 4b and Fig. 4e are shown chronopotentiograms at different current densities, showing stability for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ between 10 and $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . With the additional mass transfer channel provided by the Pt compound, the mass kinetics rate can be increased. Compared with Ce-MOF, Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05, and Ce-MOF@Pt-0.10, the Tafel slope of the OER and HER of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 is lower, at 47.9 and $188.1~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1 respectively (Fig. 4c, Fig. 4f), indicating the rapid kinetics of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 in the OER and HER process. At the same time, we have also tested the current stability under the overpotential. As can be seen in Figure S12, the current remains stable after $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ of cycling. The performance advantage of Ce$\mathsf{M O F@P t-0.15}$ is that it has a higher number of active sites, which can activate oxygen and electron changes. Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 has low HER and OER overpotentials and excellent cycling stability, mainly for the following reasons: (1) the highly porous structure of the MOF-based Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 ensures close contact between each electrocatalytic active site and the electrolyte, (2) it has a high density of active sites that can activate electronic changes, and (3) the introduction of Pt nanoparticles with high active sites in the Ce-MOF further enhances the electron transport and electrical conductivity of the material.
In order to investigate the electrochemical energy storage performance of Ce-MOF and Ce-MOF@Pt, CV curves were measured. At scanning rates from 1 to $80\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , a significant redox peak was observed in the CV curve, indicating pseudocapacitive behaviour (Fig. 5a, Figure S13a-c) [49]. With increasing scanning rate, the rapid ion transfer of ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.05}}$ was observed. GCD for the four samples was measured at different current densities within a $_{0-0.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ voltage window. When the current density of Ce-MOF is $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , the discharge time reaches 947 s, indicating that the electrochemical energy storage performance of Ce-MOF can be improved in the composite with an appropriate concentration of Pt (Fig. 5b, Figure S13d–f). Fig. 5c shows the CV curves of the different samples at a scanning rate of $5{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ . The Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 has the largest CV area, indicating that it has a higher capacitance. When the current density is $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ , the discharge time of CeMOF is the longest compared with other electrodes (Fig. 5d). In order to understand the conductivity for the different samples EIS tests were performed. Compared with the other three samples, Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 has a higher slope, which means that the electrode composed of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 can better promote the rapid transfer of electrolyte ions between the active materials. It is further proved that the composite of Ce-MOF with the appropriate concentration amount of Pt not only adds more reactive sites, but also improves the electrical conductivity of the electrode material and accelerates the ion transfer between the electrode and electrolyte (Fig. 5e). At a low current density $(1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}})$ , the specific capacitance of ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.05}}$ is as high as $1894\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , possibly due to slow Faraday and non-Faraday reactions occurring [50]. It is notable that the specific capacity of Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 is not optimal, mainly because excess Pt NPs block the ion transport channels on the surface of the Ce-MOF, affecting ion transport between electrode material and electrolyte, consistent with BET analysis. At a higher current density, ion diffusion mainly occurs in the surface region of the material, which can lead to rapid electrochemical reactions [51] (Fig. 5f).
The slope of the linear relationship between $Z^{\prime}$ and $\mathbf{\omega}_{\mathbf{\omega}}^{\mathbf{-}1/2}$ is known as the Warburg coefficient [52]. Compared with Ce-MOF, the impedance coefficient of ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.05}}$ is lower, and it can be concluded that the Pt nanoparticles composite addition to CeMOF provides an effective route for electron transport, which is beneficial to improve the stability of the energy storage cycle of the electrode (Fig. 6a). Fig. 6b shows the relationship between the frequency $(\mathsf{H}z)$ of the impedance and the corresponding phase angle $(^{\circ})$ of Ce-MOF and Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05. A phase angle of $90^{\circ}$ , represents ideal capacitor behaviour over the entire frequency range [53]. The phase angles of Ce-MOF and ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.05}}$ are $66.6^{\circ}$ and $81.3^{\circ}$ , respectively, indicating that Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 has higher capacitance performance in a lower frequency range [54]. Ce-MOF@Pt-0.05 was subjected to 3000 cycles in GCD tests. It can be clearly observed in Fig. 6c that this electrode material has excellent cycling stability, which further proves that it has excellent energy storage capacitance. After 3000 cycles the electrical capacity of the electrode material increases significantly, due to the electrolyte infiltrating into the gap of the sample during the cycle, activates the electrode material and improves the charge and discharge capacity of the materials [55,56].
A large specific surface area can provide more active sites, resulting in a significant electrochemically active surface area (ECSA). An electrochemical double capacitor (Cdl) can be obtained by CV testing with a non-Faraday range of $0.315{-}0.355\mathrm{~V}.$ Fig. 7a and Figure S14 show a quasi-rectangle and Faraday processes are not observed. Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 has a higher Cdl $15.98\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-2)}$ than other samples in the CV curve at different scan rates between 10 and $50~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{s}{\-}1$ , approximately 2.7 times that of Ce-MOF, which is consistent with the electrocatalytic activity test results (Fig. 7b). ECSA is positively correlated with the specific surface area of the active substance. A high ECSA can promote the OER and HER rates during electrocatalysis, and play an important role in reducing the Tafel slope [57]. Fig. 7c is plotted through the CV curve of the sample. The further away the slope is from zero, the greater the diffusion rate of the ions. It can be seen in Fig. 7c that the ion diffusion rate of ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.05}}$ is also the largest, consistent with the test results of supercapacitor performance. This further indicates that the introduction of an appropriate Pt can reduce the resistance of the electrode, improve the electrical conductivity of the electrode, and accelerate the diffusion of ions.
The total capacitance of the electrode materials can be separated into two process types: diffusion-controlled and capacitance-controlled. The different capacitance effects can be obtained from the linear relationship between log(i) and $\log(\upsilon)$ . The $b$ of ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.05}}$ (the slope of the linear relationship between log(i) and $\log(\nu)\dot{}$ ) is 0.34 and 0.29, respectively, indicating that the charge and discharge process of electrode materials is a diffusion-controlled process [58,59] (Fig. 7d). The contribution of different voltage scan rate to capacitance is deduced from the formula (Fig. 7e). As the scan rate increases the capacitance contribution increases significantly, from $14\%$ to $56\%$ when the scanning rate reaches $80\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , and the proportion of capacitive control gradually increases. (Fig. 7f, Figure S15).
Finally, we conducted SEM (adhesive coated material surface) and XRD analyses on ${\mathsf{C e-M O F@P t-0.05}}$ and Ce-MOF@Pt-0.15 after the cycling tests. As can be seen in Figure S16, after a long period of testing there appears to be no change in the morphology of the electrode material and the original rod-like morphology is still clearly visible, indicating that Ce-MOF@Pt has strong structural stability. Figure S17 shows the XRD patterns before and after the cycling tests. Due to the performance of the sample being tested after sticking to the nickel foam, we conduct XRD tests on the sample on the nickel foam, and the sharp peaks at $44.5^{\circ}$ and $51.9^{\circ}$ are characteristic of the nickel foam. It can be seen from the XRD patterns that there is essentially no difference in the material before and after the cycling tests, indicating that the crystal structure of the electrode material remains stable after cycling, and the crystallinity of the material does not change significantly.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, 2D Ce-MOF@Pt composites were prepared via a facile hydrothermal method. Compared with other commercial noble metal electrode materials [60,61], the large specific surface area of 2D Ce-MOF can effectively prevent the agglomeration phenomenon of Pt NPs in the reaction and reduce the cost of electrode materials. The synergy effect between Pt NPs and 2D Ce-MOF not only effectively promoted the electron transfer rate, but also increases the electrochemical reaction sites, resulting in the efficient energy conversion and storage performance of 2D CeMOF@Pt. This work provides a low cost and efficient novel strategy for the design and development of trifunctional electrode material.
